# zephyr-desktop configuration

menu "zephyr-desktop"

config ZD_TICK_MAX_MS
	int "Upper bound on the desktop loop's sleep between LVGL ticks (ms)"
	default 30
	help
	  lv_timer_handler() returns how long it is willing to sleep. Cap it so the
	  deferred reap (which runs at the top of the loop, outside LVGL dispatch)
	  cannot be starved by an idle UI.

config ZD_MAX_CLIENTS
	int "Maximum simultaneously open windows, desktop-wide"
	default 8
	help
	  Clients come from a fixed slab rather than the heap. The bound is the
	  point: a window leak shows up immediately as slab exhaustion instead of
	  as slow heap growth nobody notices.

config ZD_CLOSE_GRACE_MS
	int "How long a zapp may take to answer a close request (ms)"
	default 2000
	help
	  Clicking a window's close box sends its zapp a ZD_EV_WINDOW_CLOSE_REQUEST
	  so it can flush and close itself. This is how long the desktop waits
	  before doing it anyway.

	  The bound is what makes the event safe to send at all. Without it a zapp
	  that ignores the request -- or faults handling it -- owns a window the
	  user can no longer get rid of, which is worse than never asking. Long
	  enough for an fs_sync, not long enough to look broken.

config ZD_TASKLIST_BTN_MAX_W
	int "Widest a taskbar window button may be (px)"
	default 96
	help
	  Buttons share the strip between the launcher and the clock, so with two
	  windows open they would otherwise take half the screen each.

	  There is deliberately no matching floor. On a 320 px panel four windows
	  land near 48 px and the labels truncate, which is legible enough to click
	  the right one; a row that refused to shrink would instead push windows
	  off the end of the strip where they cannot be reached at all.

config ZD_KEY_QUEUE_LEN
	int "Key presses buffered between the input thread and the desktop loop"
	default 16
	help
	  A key arrives on Zephyr's input thread; everything it goes on to touch
	  belongs to LVGL and may only be touched from the desktop loop. So keys
	  are queued, exactly as LVGL's own pointer driver queues pointer
	  samples.

	  Overflowing drops the key and logs it. Blocking would stall the input
	  thread behind a UI that is not drawing, and growing would turn a stuck
	  desktop into an out-of-memory one. Sixteen is several seconds of fast
	  typing at a 30 ms loop.

config ZD_TEXT_MAX
	int "Largest document a text widget will hold (bytes)"
	default 4096
	help
	  The cap on one editable field, enforced by the widget itself, and the
	  size of the single scratch buffer that makes deleting a selection one
	  pass instead of one memmove per character.

	  A bounded editor is not an apology. Notepad 1.0 topped out somewhere
	  near 54 KB and said so; what matters is that the limit is stated and
	  the zapp is told when a file will not fit, rather than the document
	  being silently truncated.

config ZD_MAX_MENUS
	int "Maximum simultaneously live menu bars and drop-downs, desktop-wide"
	default 16
	help
	  Counts bars and drop-downs together: a window with File and Edit uses
	  three. Fixed array, same reasoning as windows and open files.

config ZD_MENU_MAX_SUBMENUS
	int "Maximum drop-downs on one menu bar"
	default 5
	help
	  Bounded by the panel, not by taste. Five titles at touch sizing is
	  already most of a 320 px bar, and a title pushed off the end cannot be
	  reached at all.

config ZD_MENU_MAX_ITEMS
	int "Maximum items in one drop-down, separators included"
	default 12
	help
	  Notepad's File menu is five items and a separator. Twelve leaves room
	  without making the record large -- each item carries its own label,
	  because a menu outlives the string the zapp built it from.

config ZD_CLIPBOARD_MAX
	int "Largest thing the clipboard will hold (bytes)"
	default 2048
	help
	  Static, not heap: the clipboard outlives every zapp by design, so a
	  heap allocation here would be one the desktop never frees and could
	  fail at the worst possible moment -- the instant after Ctrl+C, where
	  the user has no reason to imagine it could not work. A fixed buffer
	  always has room for something.

	  A copy larger than this is truncated and logged, not refused. Half a
	  paste is recoverable; a copy that silently produced nothing is not.

config ZD_MAX_TEXTS
	int "Maximum simultaneously live text widgets, desktop-wide"
	default 8
	help
	  Fixed array, for the reason windows and open files are: a leak should
	  hit a wall that names itself rather than grow the heap quietly.

config ZD_MAX_LISTS
	int "Maximum simultaneously live list widgets, desktop-wide"
	default 4
	help
	  Two browsers and a file picker is three; four leaves one spare. Fixed
	  array, for the same reason windows, text widgets and open files are.

config ZD_LIST_ITEMS_TOTAL
	int "Rows shared between every live list"
	default 96
	help
	  One pool for the whole desktop rather than an array per list, because
	  a per-list array is mostly idle memory: four lists that could each
	  hold ZD_LIST_MAX_ITEMS rows would reserve twenty kilobytes to show a
	  directory of nine files. Same shape as the open-file table -- one
	  bounded resource, plus a per-list cap so no one list can take it all.

	  A row costs ZD_NAME_MAX bytes and is not separately configurable: a
	  row that cannot hold a filename cannot show a directory, so the two
	  are one constant by construction.

config ZD_LIST_MAX_ITEMS
	int "Most rows any one list will hold"
	default 64
	help
	  Reported to zapps through list_get_capacity(); adding past it returns
	  -ENOSPC rather than silently dropping the row, so a zapp can say "this
	  directory has more in it" instead of quietly lying. A zapp must never
	  read this symbol directly -- see CLAUDE.md.

config ZD_MAX_GRIDS
	int "Maximum simultaneously live cell grids, desktop-wide"
	default 4
	help
	  A game with a board and a face button is two; two games at once are
	  four. Fixed array, for the reason windows, lists and open files are.

config ZD_GRID_CELLS_TOTAL
	int "Cells shared between every live grid"
	default 768
	help
	  One pool for the whole desktop, exactly as ZD_LIST_ITEMS_TOTAL is, and
	  for the same reason -- four grids each able to hold a full board would
	  reserve most of the spare .bss on a board with 512 KB of it to draw a
	  nine-by-nine.

	  Unlike list rows, cells are taken a whole grid at a time and are
	  indexed arithmetically, so this is carved into contiguous blocks
	  rather than chained. A cell costs eight bytes, so the default is six
	  kilobytes.

config ZD_GRID_MAX_CELLS
	int "Most cells any one grid may have"
	default 480
	help
	  Reported to zapps through grid_get_capacity(); creating a larger grid
	  is refused rather than silently clipped. 480 is Minesweeper's Expert
	  board, which is the largest thing anybody has asked for and does not
	  come close to fitting on any panel here -- the pixels run out first,
	  which is what grid_fit() is for.

	  A zapp must never read this symbol directly; see CLAUDE.md.

config ZD_NET
	bool "Networking for zapps (ABI 0.8 http_request)"
	select NETWORKING
	select NET_IPV4
	select NET_TCP
	select NET_SOCKETS
	select NET_SOCKETS_POSIX_NAMES if !POSIX_API
	select DNS_RESOLVER
	select NET_DHCPV4
	help
	  Adds the 0.8 http_request/http_read/http_release calls and the
	  ZD_EV_HTTP_RESPONSE event.

	  Plain HTTP only. There is deliberately no TLS: the desktop would need
	  mbedTLS and a trust store to talk to anything on the public internet,
	  which is a lot of flash and a lot of expiry management for a demo.
	  The clippy zapp reaches its Hugging Face Space through tools/hf-proxy.py
	  on the development host, which terminates TLS there. See docs/clippy.md.

	  Off by default. A desktop that does not need the network should not
	  carry a TCP stack.

config ZD_HTTP_MAX_REQUESTS
	int "Concurrent zapp HTTP requests"
	depends on ZD_NET
	default 2
	range 1 8
	help
	  Each slot holds a whole response body, so this multiplies
	  ZD_HTTP_BODY_MAX. Two is enough for one zapp asking a question while
	  another is still waiting.

config ZD_HTTP_BODY_MAX
	int "Largest response body, bytes"
	depends on ZD_NET
	default 8192
	help
	  Responses longer than this are truncated. The buffer is statically
	  allocated per slot, so this is real RAM whether or not anything uses
	  the network.

config ZD_HTTP_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Seconds to wait for a response"
	depends on ZD_NET
	default 120
	help
	  The assistant Space runs a model on CPU and can take well over a
	  minute. This is generous on purpose; the request is on a worker
	  thread, so waiting costs the desktop nothing.

config ZD_HTTP_STACK_SIZE
	int "HTTP worker thread stack"
	depends on ZD_NET
	default 4096

config ZD_MAX_TIMERS
	int "Maximum simultaneously running zapp timers, desktop-wide"
	default 4
	help
	  Each slot costs one lv_timer, created on first use and thereafter
	  paused rather than deleted -- see app/src/host/timer_api.c for why
	  that is a safety property and not a shortcut.

config ZD_TIMER_MIN_MS
	int "Shortest period a zapp timer will actually run at (ms)"
	default 50
	help
	  Zapps run as callbacks on the thread that draws the screen, so a zapp
	  asking to be woken every millisecond is asking the desktop to stop
	  repainting. A shorter period is raised to this rather than refused:
	  the caller wanted "often", and can have as much of it as is safe.

	  This is not a frame clock and there is deliberately no way to ask for
	  one.

config ZD_TEXT_PAGE_LINES
	int "Lines Page Up and Page Down move the caret"
	default 8
	help
	  A real editor pages by the height of the visible area. A text widget
	  has no cheap way to ask LVGL how many lines that is right now, so this
	  is a fixed count -- close enough on every panel this project targets,
	  and honest about being a constant.

config ZD_FS_ROOT
	string "Mount point of the app filesystem"
	default "/RAM:"
	help
	  FATFS requires a mount point of the form "/<VOLUME>:", where VOLUME is
	  generated from a devicetree disk-name. Under QEMU that is the RAM disk;
	  an SD-backed board would use its own volume. Apps never see this value
	  -- they resolve directories through the session -- so changing it per
	  board costs nothing above the mount table.

config ZD_MAX_WINDOWS_PER_ZAPP
	int "Maximum windows a single app instance may own"
	default 4

config ZD_MAX_OPEN_FILES
	int "Maximum simultaneously open files, desktop-wide"
	default 8
	help
	  Open files come from a fixed array for the same reason windows do: a
	  zapp that forgets to close should hit a wall that says so, not grow the
	  heap quietly. Each entry costs a struct fs_file_t.

config ZD_MAX_OPEN_DIRS
	int "Maximum simultaneously open directories, desktop-wide"
	default 4
	help
	  Directories are only held open while something is being listed, so the
	  bound can be far tighter than the file one.

config ZD_MAX_OPEN_PER_ZAPP
	int "Maximum open files and directories a single zapp instance may hold"
	default 4
	help
	  Counts files and directories together. Mirrors
	  ZD_MAX_WINDOWS_PER_ZAPP: one instance must not be able to starve the
	  rest of the desktop of a shared, bounded resource.

config ZD_FS_IO_CHUNK
	int "Largest number of bytes one zapp read or write may move"
	default 4096
	help
	  Zapps run as callbacks on the desktop thread, so a transfer stops the
	  whole UI while it runs -- and on a board where storage and the display
	  share a pin (see ZD_SPI_DC_MISO_ARBITER) the screen physically cannot
	  be drawn during one. Capping a single call caps the stall.

	  Reads and writes are therefore allowed to be short, which the ABI
	  states as a contract rather than an implementation detail: zapps loop.

config ZD_FS_AUTOFORMAT
	bool "Format the app volume if it has no filesystem"
	default y
	help
	  Enforced with FS_MOUNT_FLAG_NO_FORMAT on the mount, NOT via Kconfig:
	  FS_FATFS_MOUNT_MKFS defaults to y and *selects* FS_FATFS_MKFS, so
	  setting either to n in a board fragment is silently overridden. The
	  mount flag is the only thing fat_fs.c actually checks.

	  Right for a RAM disk that is blank at every boot. Wrong for removable
	  media, where a failed mount means "unreadable card", not "please
	  destroy it".

config ZD_SEED_BUILTIN_ZAPPS
	bool "Install the built-in zapps into /system/zapps at boot"
	default y
	help
	  Writes the .llext files embedded in the image to the filesystem if what
	  is there does not match them byte for byte. A stand-in for an installer
	  on targets whose storage is blank at every boot, such as the QEMU RAM
	  disk.

	  On a board with persistent storage it is a per-board judgement, and the
	  two hardware targets here answer it differently on purpose:

	  mimxrt1060_evk has it OFF. That checkpoint exists to prove a .llext can
	  arrive as a genuinely external file, so the desktop must find zapps it
	  did not write itself; seeding would make the check vacuous.

	  m5stack_cores3 has it ON. Seeding is itself a write to a card that
	  shares a pin with the display, so it exercises the arbiter on every
	  boot, and it means a plain FAT-formatted card boots to a populated
	  desktop rather than an empty one. External delivery is still checkable
	  there: delete a zapp from the card and copy your own in.

config ZD_ZAPP_LOAD_VIA_BUFFER
	bool "Read a zapp into RAM before loading it, instead of streaming from the file"
	default y if LLEXT_STORAGE_WRITABLE
	help
	  llext_fs_loader streams the ELF straight off the filesystem, which is
	  what we want: the file is the source of truth and nothing has to fit in
	  RAM twice.

	  Xtensa cannot do that. It requires CONFIG_LLEXT_STORAGE_WRITABLE, which
	  in turn requires a loader implementing peek() -- and fs_loader sets
	  .peek = NULL. So on those targets the zapp is read into a heap buffer
	  first and handed to llext_buf_loader, which the loader may modify in
	  place during linking. The buffer therefore has to stay allocated until
	  the zapp is unloaded.

	  Discovery is unaffected either way: the .llext is still a real file the
	  desktop found on a real filesystem.

config ZD_LLEXT_HEAP_REGION
	bool "Put the llext heap in a named linker region instead of ordinary RAM"
	depends on LLEXT
	select LLEXT_HEAP_DYNAMIC
	help
	  By default llext carves its heap out of the same SRAM as everything
	  else, sized by CONFIG_LLEXT_HEAP_SIZE. That works wherever SRAM is the
	  only memory there is.

	  It does not work on a board whose SRAM is already spoken for. The
	  pimoroni_presto is the case this exists for: 520 KB of SRAM, 450 KB of
	  which is the display's framebuffer, and the framebuffer cannot move --
	  it is scanned out continuously by DMA, and the 8 MB of PSRAM it might
	  otherwise live in is reached over the QMI bus shared with flash XIP,
	  which underruns. So the framebuffer stays and the llext heap moves.

	  Zapp *code* runs from this heap, so the region has to be executable.
	  That is free on the Presto because the MPU is off there, but a board
	  that turns CONFIG_ARM_MPU on must give the region an executable
	  attribute (ATTR_MPU_EXTMEM, not ATTR_MPU_RAM) or every zapp will fault
	  on its first instruction.

config ZD_LLEXT_HEAP_REGION_NAME
	string "Linker region to place the llext heap in"
	default "PSRAM"
	depends on ZD_LLEXT_HEAP_REGION
	help
	  Must match a zephyr,memory-region name in the devicetree. The buffer is
	  placed with Z_GENERIC_SECTION, which is the same mechanism LVGL's own
	  pool uses (CONFIG_LV_Z_MEMORY_POOL_ZEPHYR_REGION_NAME) -- and, usefully,
	  one that leaves the buffer out of the .bss clear at boot, so nothing
	  touches the memory before the driver backing it has come up.

config ZD_LLEXT_HEAP_REGION_KB
	int "Size of the llext heap when it lives in a linker region"
	default 384
	depends on ZD_LLEXT_HEAP_REGION
	help
	  Kilobytes. The equivalent of CONFIG_LLEXT_HEAP_SIZE, which is not used
	  in this mode -- it depends on !LLEXT_HEAP_DYNAMIC, so setting it here
	  would warn and do nothing, which is exactly the class of silent no-op
	  the board fragments warn about elsewhere.

config ZD_TOUCH_SLOP_PX
	int "Extra pixels of hit area around small controls"
	default 0
	help
	  Win95 chrome is mouse-sized: the launcher button is 20 px tall at the
	  very bottom of the screen, and the window close box is 14x14. A cursor
	  hits those exactly; a fingertip does not.

	  This expands the *clickable* area of the small controls without moving
	  or redrawing anything, so the retro geometry is preserved pixel for
	  pixel and only the hit test grows. Leave at 0 for pointer-driven
	  targets; set it to roughly half a fingertip on touch panels.

config ZD_OSK
	bool "On-screen keyboard"
	default y
	select LV_USE_BUTTONMATRIX
	help
	  A soft keyboard on the overlay layer, toggled from the taskbar. It
	  feeds the same funnel a real keyboard does (app/src/input/keys.h), so
	  nothing downstream can tell the two apart.

	  Not a convenience. The CoreS3 is a 320x240 touch panel with no keys
	  at all and is the only target this project has run on silicon;
	  without this, every zapp that needs typing is QEMU-only.

	  Costs about 128 px of a 240 px screen while raised, which is why it
	  is a toggle and not a permanent strip.

config ZD_OSK_AUTO
	bool "Raise the on-screen keyboard when something wants typing"
	default y if ZD_TOUCH_SLOP_PX != 0
	depends on ZD_OSK
	help
	  Pops the keyboard up when a text widget takes the caret, so that on a
	  touch panel the field you just tapped can be typed into without a
	  separate trip to the taskbar.

	  Defaults on exactly where the touch slop says there is a finger and
	  therefore presumably no keyboard. On a target with a real one this
	  would be a large window covering the screen for no reason.

config ZD_TOUCH_PROBE
	bool "Poll the touch controller directly and log every result"
	depends on I2C
	help
	  Debug aid for a touch panel that is present on I2C but produces no
	  input events. The touch driver returns silently on an I2C error, so
	  "no events" and "every read failed" look identical from outside. This
	  reads the controller's touch-count register from a thread of our own
	  and reports errors and counts, which tells the two apart.

	  Off by default; it duplicates the driver's own bus traffic.

config ZD_SELFTEST
	bool "Run the permission and handle checks at boot"
	default y
	help
	  Exercises the paths that must refuse: traversal, out-of-root access,
	  writes to the read-only system root, stale handles and cross-app
	  handles. A shim that silently accepts everything looks exactly like one
	  that works, so these run on the target rather than in a test directory.

config ZD_SMOKE_TEST
	bool "Launch and close every zapp at boot, then check for leaks"
	help
	  Drives the desktop from the loop rather than from a pointer: discover,
	  launch everything, let it settle, ask every window to close, wait out
	  the grace period, and compare the client, handle, open-file and
	  instance counts against the values captured at boot.

	  This is the only way the load/unload path can be exercised on a
	  headless run, and the only automated check that a zapp still loads at
	  all. Milestone J hand-tested a stale binary twice for want of it.

	  Off by default: it takes over the desktop for a few seconds and closes
	  every window it finds. Turn it on for a build you are measuring.

config ZD_SMOKE_ROUNDS
	int "Launch/close cycles the smoke test runs"
	default 2
	depends on ZD_SMOKE_TEST
	help
	  One round proves it works; the second proves it can be done again,
	  which is the round that catches a resource freed to the wrong place.

config ZD_SPI_DC_MISO_ARBITER
	bool "Take the shared SPI MISO pin back from the display for storage"
	depends on DISK_DRIVER_SDMMC && PINCTRL && LV_Z_LVGL_MUTEX
	depends on ZD_ZAPP_LOAD_VIA_BUFFER
	select PINCTRL_NON_STATIC
	help
	  For boards that wire one pin to both SPI MISO and the display's
	  data/command line -- the M5Stack CoreS3 does this with GPIO35. The
	  display driver claims the pin as an output at init and the card can
	  then never answer, so every filesystem operation has to borrow it
	  back and give it up again. See app/src/host/bus_arb.c.

	  Depends on ZD_ZAPP_LOAD_VIA_BUFFER because the arbiter can only
	  bracket filesystem calls this project makes itself. The llext fs
	  loader reads the ELF from inside llext_load(), where there is nothing
	  to wrap; the buffer loader reads the file here first.

config LLEXT_EXPORT_SYMBOL_GROUP_DESKTOP
	bool "Export the zephyr-desktop host API to extensions"
	default y
	depends on LLEXT
	help
	  Gates EXPORT_GROUP_SYMBOL(DESKTOP, ...). Exactly one symbol lives in
	  this group -- zd_get_host_api() -- because everything else an app can
	  reach, it reaches through the returned vtable.

module = ZD
module-str = zephyr-desktop
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
